#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4055cd-dc46-58cf-9414-ebd3aeced26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504052} {:answer/id #uuid "169a9454-865f-5c99-afad-24018fd51b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504072} {:answer/id #uuid "24013cd6-fd21-5ea4-a444-92864f2d2b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504094} {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} {:answer/id #uuid "2fefcbb7-1707-5b33-9ea0-368b42c461ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c7ddcb-f4c6-55ee-80f6-45bc59cf1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504096} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba947109-a6e8-5648-9e45-f1bf117b385d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93366598-9424-5753-a6aa-f85435a0ef2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130279" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446186} {:answer/id #uuid "78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446204} {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446207} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" :question/math-academy-id "q-130806" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446194} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} {:answer/id #uuid "94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446213} {:answer/id #uuid "c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446221} {:answer/id #uuid "c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446211} :answer-field/id #uuid "716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130811" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446193} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446216} {:answer/id #uuid "b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446218} {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446225} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36306" :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi +e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446179} {:answer/id #uuid "1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{4}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446180} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446208} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi +e}{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446198} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aca3088-e8f0-5dbc-95a5-523c9e4320d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ea3ebc5-2c81-5696-aab4-931973a19233" :question/math-academy-id "q-37074" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(e,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(e,\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}{e},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{e},\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\cdot (-1)}{e},\\,1\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction of $∇g(e,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(e,\\pi )‖ & =‖⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb6012-956b-5c39-8935-74645f89ee82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i+2\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500483} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0516fc-fa33-5a32-bc85-021583caaccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500790} {:answer/id #uuid "a91d4692-51f1-5ef6-96a3-68a85bcad337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500817} {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c25011-b246-57b2-afb4-9949bd083e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,i+\\pi \\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20c5f55-306b-5cef-9a67-804b11be245c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500840} :answer-field/id #uuid "941d1058-20a9-5c97-a5b7-7c5da913add0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" :question/math-academy-id "q-83562" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e252ff-bff2-5a0c-8722-678c22a7c16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517501} {:answer/id #uuid "070f81d7-bbdd-59f5-971a-c1e85fd656fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517503} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8dff85-9179-52c1-a1eb-e2b80b8d392e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517603} {:answer/id #uuid "636cdbc8-2c6a-553b-a040-1b1eb24c4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517671} {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6bc3a1-b754-5de6-a15a-17b5690dd1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86533" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} :answer-field/id #uuid "082380e4-f20f-513e-9d31-b936b5054d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" :question/math-academy-id "q-86540" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$"}]}
